  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity builds through the end of the\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSouthwest winds bring the heat to the region starting Wednesday with \nthe warmest day over the north being tomorrow. Temps will climb to \naround 90 over inland locations with locations along the coast near \n80. Dewpoints will climb into the 60s though may be able to mix out \ninto the 50s during the afternoon. This should help keep apparent \ntemps near air temperatures tomorrow. Temperatures stay \nelevated through Saturday. Seabreeze develops Wednesday \nevening, to help reduce temperatures along the Downeast coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the northwest, moving into \nNew Brunswick. Associated cold front is trending slower, as it \nswings through on Friday evening, through Maine by Saturday. \nAnother low pressure system to the southwest comes in as well, \nproviding another round of rain this weekend. There is some \ninstability ahead of the cold front. NAM has begun to pick up on\nthe system, with it currently showing around 1,500 \u2013 2,000 J/kg\nof CAPE, good low level lapse rates (roughly 8C/km), and plenty\nof precipitation. Soundings are moist, with classic long and \nskinny CAPE throughout most of northern and eastern Maine. PWAT \nvalues around 1.8\u201d possible, which is around the 90th \npercentile, approaching the 1.9\u201d daytime maximum value on\nFriday, and 1.7\" record on Saturday. Keeping the threat of \nthunder inland, as a seabreeze off of the stable Gulf of Maine \nwaters should stabilize the coast and keep daytime high \ntemperatures cooler than inland areas. Storm motions, based on \nCorfidi vectors, seems to be moving fast enough to keep flash \nflood threat at bay, but will need to see how this evolves as \nthe regional models and CAMs pick up on the system and \npreexisting environment. \n\nGlobal models are keep rainfall through the state and continue \nto bring instability in along the Central Highlands and Downeast\nregion on Saturday. North has less instability. Chance for \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon as front makes its way through.\nConvective showers could bring periods of heavier rainfall.",
